That's a normal Power Apps gallery behavior.

A gallery always has a Selected record, and when the screen loads, Power Apps automatically selects the first row.

If you want no row highlighted until the user clicks one, don't use ThisItem.IsSelected directly. Instead, use a variable.


---

Step 1 - Create a Variable for the Selected Supplier

On your screen's OnVisible property:

Set(varSelectedSupplier, Blank())

This means no supplier is selected when the screen opens.


---

Step 2 - Store the Clicked Row

Inside Gallery_Suppliers_New, select the control that users click (Supplier Name label, a button, icon, or transparent rectangle).

Set its OnSelect:

Set(
    varSelectedSupplier,
    ThisItem.ID
)

If your list doesn't have a reliable ID, you can use:

Set(
    varSelectedSupplier,
    ThisItem.SupplierCode
)

or another unique field.


---

Step 3 - Change TemplateFill

Instead of:

If(
    ThisItem.IsSelected,
    RGBA(220,235,255,1),
    Color.White
)

Use:

If(
    ThisItem.ID = varSelectedSupplier,
    RGBA(220,235,255,1),
    Color.White
)


---

Step 4 - Update the Left Selection Bar

If you created rect_Selected, change its Visible property to:

ThisItem.ID = varSelectedSupplier

instead of:

ThisItem.IsSelected


---

Result

When Screen1 opens:

AOI
Threebond
Resonac

No row is highlighted.

After clicking AOI:

[Blue] AOI
       Threebond
       Resonac

After clicking Resonac:

AOI
       Threebond
[Blue] Resonac

Only the clicked row is highlighted.


---

Bonus: Clear Selection

If you have a Reset or Clear button, you can remove the highlight by setting:

Set(varSelectedSupplier, Blank())

This returns the gallery to a state where no row is highlighted. This approach is commonly used in enterprise Power Apps because it gives you full control over selection behavior rather than relying on the gallery's built-in Selected record.



