In Power Apps, how can I make a gallery’s template row height automatically adjust based on the content length of the data, especially for fields with long text (e.g., descriptions)?


****


In Power Apps, a gallery does not truly auto-expand its template height dynamically per row like HTML/CSS.
However, you can achieve a “dynamic row height” effect using these techniques:


---

Recommended Approach (Best Practice)

Use:

Flexible Height Gallery instead of a standard Vertical Gallery

Labels with:

AutoHeight = true

Dynamic Height



This is the correct solution for long descriptions/comments.


---

Step-by-Step Solution

1. Insert a Flexible Height Gallery

Instead of:

Vertical Gallery


Use:

Flexible height gallery


Path:

Insert → Gallery → Flexible height

Why?

A normal gallery uses one fixed template height for all rows.

A flexible height gallery allows each row to expand depending on content.


---

2. Add Your Long Text Label

Example:

lbl_Description

Set:

Text = ThisItem.Description


---

3. Enable Auto Height

Set:

AutoHeight = true

This allows the label itself to grow vertically.


---

4. Important: Remove Fixed Height

Do NOT use a hardcoded height like:

Height = 40

Instead use:

Height = Self.TextHeight

OR simply leave it because AutoHeight manages it.


---

5. Set Width Properly

This is VERY important.

Example:

Width = Parent.TemplateWidth - 20

If TemplateWidth causes error:

Use:

Width = Parent.Width - 20

because in some controls:

TemplateWidth is not recognized

Parent.Width is safer


This matches the error you encountered previously.


---

6. Position Controls Dynamically

If you have another control below the description:

Example:

lbl_Status

Set:

Y = lbl_Description.Y + lbl_Description.Height + 5

This makes controls move downward automatically when the description becomes longer.


---

7. Set Gallery Template Size

For Flexible Height Gallery:

Do NOT rely heavily on fixed TemplateSize.

Set something small like:

TemplateSize = 80

The gallery will expand automatically per row.


---

Example Layout

Inside gallery:

Description Label

Text = ThisItem.Description
AutoHeight = true
Width = Parent.Width - 20

Status Label Below

Text = ThisItem.Status
Y = lbl_Description.Y + lbl_Description.Height + 5


---

Common Problem

Problem:

Text overlaps other controls

Cause:

Controls below are using fixed Y positions.

Fix:

Use dynamic Y formulas based on previous control height.

Example:

Y = PreviousControl.Y + PreviousControl.Height + 5


---

Recommended Structure

Flexible Height Gallery
 ├── Title Label
 ├── Description Label (AutoHeight=true)
 ├── Status Label
 └── Date Label

Each lower control references the previous control’s height.


---

If You Already Use a Normal Vertical Gallery

You have 2 options:

Option 1 (Recommended)

Replace it with:

Flexible Height Gallery


Option 2

Manually calculate row height using formulas, but this is harder and less reliable.


---

Performance Tip

If descriptions are VERY long and many rows exist:

Avoid too many AutoHeight controls

Use pagination/filtering

Keep gallery optimized


Because flexible galleries can become slower with huge datasets.


---

Best Practice for SharePoint + Power Apps

For comments/descriptions:

Use:

Multiple lines of text in SharePoint

Flexible Height Gallery in Power Apps


This combination works best.

